1. Carmine (#FF004D in RGB, 0, 100, 70, 0 in CMYK) - This deep shade of red has been associated with love and passion in many cultures throughout history. In ancient Egypt, carmine was used to decorate the tombs of pharaohs and symbolized the power of life and rebirth. In China, it was believed to bring good luck and fortune.
2. Garnet (#733635 in RGB, 0, 55, 70, 60 in CMYK) - Named after the precious stone, garnet is a dark red hue that has been associated with protection and strength. In ancient Rome, garnet was believed to protect its wearer from harm and was often worn as a talisman. In Hinduism, garnet is associated with the root chakra, which is believed to provide a sense of grounding and stability.
3. Maroon (#800000 in RGB, 0, 100, 100, 50 in CMYK) - A rich, dark red color, maroon is often associated with luxury and sophistication. In Japan, maroon is a traditional color worn by samurai warriors and symbolizes courage and bravery. In Christianity, maroon is often used in liturgical vestments and represents the blood of Christ.
4. Scarlet (#FF2400 in RGB, 0, 90, 100, 0 in CMYK) - A bright, vibrant shade of red, scarlet has been associated with passion and desire in many cultures. In ancient Greece, scarlet was often used to dye clothing and represented the power of love. In Hinduism, scarlet is associated with the goddess Kali, who is often depicted wearing a scarlet sari.
5. Vermilion (#E34234 in RGB, 0, 80, 90, 10 in CMYK) - A bright, fiery shade of red, vermilion has been associated with vitality and energy in many cultures. In ancient China, vermilion was often used to paint the doors of houses and symbolized good fortune and prosperity. In medieval Europe, vermilion was used in illuminated manuscripts and represented the blood of martyrs. <|endoftext|>
