How can I create a Ruby method that uses computer vision to detect objects in an image and return the coordinates of the detected objects? Can you provide an example using the OpenCV library?
```ruby
require 'opencv'
def detect_objects(image_path)
 image = OpenCV::IplImage.load(image_path)
 gray_image = image.BGR2GRAY
 classifier = OpenCV::CvHaarClassifierCascade::load('path/to/cascade/classifier.xml')
 objects = classifier.detect_objects(gray_image)
 coordinates = []
 objects.each do |obj|
 coordinates << [obj.top_left.x, obj.top_left.y, obj.bottom_right.x, obj.bottom_right.y]
 end
 return coordinates
end
# Example usage:
coordinates = detect_objects('path/to/image.jpg')
puts coordinates.inspect
```
<|end|>
<|asst|>
This code should work for detecting objects in an image using the OpenCV library in Ruby. The `detect_objects` method takes an `image_path` parameter as input and returns an array of coordinates for each detected object in the image. 
First, the method loads the image using `OpenCV::IplImage.load` and converts it to grayscale using `BGR2GRAY`. Then, it loads the cascade classifier from an XML file using `OpenCV::CvHaarClassifierCascade::load`.
The `detect_objects` method then uses the `detect_objects` method of the classifier to detect objects in the image. This method returns an array of `CvRect` objects, which represent the bounding boxes of the detected objects.
Finally, the method loops through each `CvRect` object and extracts the coordinates of the top-left and bottom-right corners of the bounding box. These coordinates are added to the `coordinates` array, which is then returned by the method.
To use this method, simply call it with the path to the image you want to detect objects in, like this:
```ruby
coordinates = detect_objects('path/to/image.jpg')
puts coordinates.inspect
```
This will print out an array of coordinates for each detected object in the image. <|endoftext|>
