Sure, here's an example of how to use the Google Cloud Vision API to detect text in an image using Ruby:
```ruby
require "google/cloud/vision"

def detect_text(path)
 image_annotator = Google::Cloud::Vision::ImageAnnotator.new

 response = image_annotator.text_detection image: path
 text = response.responses.first.text_annotations.first.description

 return text
end

# Example usage:
text = detect_text "path/to/image.jpg"
puts text
```

This code uses the Google Cloud Vision Ruby client library to create an `ImageAnnotator` object. The `detect_text` method takes a file path as input and sends the image to the API using the `text_detection` method of the `ImageAnnotator`.

The API response returns a list of `TextAnnotation` objects. In this case, we only need the first annotation which should contain the entire detected text. Therefore, we call `response.responses.first.text_annotations.first.description` to extract the text.

Finally, the extracted text is returned by the method and can be printed out or further processed as needed. <|endoftext|>
