What is a stored procedure in SQL?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A stored procedure is a precompiled set of SQL statements that can be executed with a single command. Stored procedures are often used to encapsulate complex business logic or frequently used queries, which can improve performance and maintainability of an application. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is normalization in SQL and why is it important?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database so that it eliminates redundancy and dependencies among tables. It is important because it helps to ensure data consistency and accuracy, as well as reduce storage requirements. Normalization also helps to prevent update anomalies and makes it easier to modify the database schema. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between clustered and non-clustered indexes in SQL?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In SQL, a clustered index determines the physical order of data in a table, whereas a non-clustered index is a separate data structure that stores a copy of selected columns of a table, sorted by the indexed column(s). Clustered indexes are typically used for primary keys or frequently queried columns, while non-clustered indexes are used to speed up searches on other columns. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


