The French word for "book" is "livre". The word "livre" comes from the Latin word "liber", which means "book" or "parchment". The usage of "livre" in contemporary French literature is very common, as it is the most commonly used word for "book" in the French language.
In terms of comparative analysis with other Romance languages, the equivalent word for "book" in Italian is "libro", in Spanish it is "libro", and in Portuguese it is "livro". As we can see, the Spanish and Italian words for "book" are very similar to the French word "livre", as they all come from the same Latin root "liber". The Portuguese word "livro" is also similar, but it has a slightly different pronunciation.
Here's a sample sentence in French that uses the word "livre" in context: "J'ai acheté un livre intéressant à la librairie." This translates to "I bought an interesting book at the bookstore." <|endoftext|>
