<|user|>
I have a list of temperatures recorded in Celsius, Fahrenheit and Kelvin units for different cities. The temperatures are stored in a database table named "City Temperatures" with the following structure:
| City Name | Temperature (C) | Temperature (F) | Temperature (K) |
|-----------|----------------|----------------|----------------|
| City A | 10 | 50 | 283.15 |
| City B | -5 | 23 | 268.15 |
| City C | 28 | 82 | 301.15 |
| City D | 15 | 59 | 288.15 |
I want to convert all the temperatures from Celsius to Kelvin. Can you write a formula for me to achieve this in Excel? Using the formula, what would be the temperature in Kelvin for City A?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! To convert Celsius to Kelvin, you simply need to add 273.15 to the Celsius temperature.
In Excel, you can use the following formula to convert the temperature in Celsius to Kelvin:
`=Celsius Temperature + 273.15`
For example, to convert the temperature of City A from Celsius to Kelvin, you would use the formula:
`=10+273.15`
This would give you the temperature in Kelvin for City A, which is:
`283.15` 
So the temperature in Kelvin for City A would be 283.15. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Thank you for the formula. How do I convert the temperatures from Fahrenheit to Celsius in Excel?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To convert Fahrenheit to Celsius, you can use the following formula in Excel:
`= (Fahrenheit Temperature - 32) * 5/9`
For example, to convert the temperature of City A from Fahrenheit to Celsius, you would use the formula:
`= (50 - 32) * 5/9`
This would give you the temperature in Celsius for City A, which is:
`10` <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
That's helpful. Can you also help me find out the average temperature for all the cities in Fahrenheit?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! To find the average temperature for all the cities in Fahrenheit, you can use the `AVERAGE` function in Excel.
You can select the range of temperatures in Fahrenheit for all the cities (i.e., cell range B2:B5 in your table), and then use the formula:
`=AVERAGE(B2:B5)`
This will give you the average temperature for all the cities in Fahrenheit. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Great, thanks! Is there a way to determine which city has the highest temperature in Kelvin using Excel?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use the `MAX` function in Excel to determine which city has the highest temperature in Kelvin. 
First, you need to select the range of temperatures in Kelvin for all the cities (i.e., cell range D2:D5 in your table), and then use the formula:
`=MAX(D2:D5)`
This will give you the highest temperature in Kelvin among all the cities. If you want to know which city has this highest temperature, you can use the `INDEX` and `MATCH` functions together with the `MAX` function. For example, you can use the following formula:
`=INDEX(A2:A5,MATCH(MAX(D2:D5),D2:D5,0))`
This will give you the name of the city that has the highest temperature in Kelvin. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


