<|asst|>
You can use the same loop we used earlier to find the index of the specific mountain range and then use that index to access the corresponding elevation in the elevations array. Once you have all the elevations for that mountain range, you can use the Array.Max and Array.Min methods to find the highest and lowest elevations, respectively. Here's the C# code to accomplish this:
```
string[] mountainRanges = { "Rocky Mountains", "Appalachian Mountains", "Sierra Nevada Mountains" };
int[] elevations = { 14264, 6684, 14494 };
string targetRange = "Rocky Mountains";
int highestElevation = 0;
int lowestElevation = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < mountainRanges.Length; i++)
{
 if (mountainRanges[i] == targetRange)
 {
 highestElevation = elevations[i];
 lowestElevation = elevations[i];
 }
}
foreach (int elevation in elevations)
{
 if (elevation > highestElevation && mountainRanges[Array.IndexOf(elevations, elevation)] == targetRange)
 {
 highestElevation = elevation;
 }
 else if (elevation < lowestElevation && mountainRanges[Array.IndexOf(elevations, elevation)] == targetRange)
 {
 lowestElevation = elevation;
 }
}
Console.WriteLine("The highest elevation in the " + targetRange + " is: " + highestElevation);
Console.WriteLine("The lowest elevation in the " + targetRange + " is: " + lowestElevation);
```
This code will output the highest and lowest elevations for the specified mountain range. <|endoftext|>
