<|user|>
I have a puzzle for you. Can you modify this C++ code to add a function that calculates the sum of two numbers, but without using the + operator? The main function already has two variables, num1 and num2, set to 5 and 10 respectively. Your task is to add a function that calculates the sum of these two numbers and returns the result. However, you cannot use the + operator or any other arithmetic operator in your implementation. Good luck!
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Certainly! Here's a modified version of the code that uses bitwise operators to calculate the sum of two numbers without using the '+' operator:
```
#include 
using namespace std;
int add(int num1, int num2) {
 int carry;
 while (num2 != 0) {
 carry = num1 & num2;
 num1 = num1 ^ num2;
 num2 = carry << 1;
 }
 return num1;
}
int main() {
 int num1 = 5, num2 = 10;
 int sum = add(num1, num2);
 cout << "Sum of " << num1 << " and " << num2 << " is: " << sum << endl;
 return 0;
}
```
This implementation uses the bitwise AND and XOR operators to perform the addition. The carry is calculated using the AND operator, and the sum is calculated using the XOR operator. The carry is then shifted one bit to the left and added to the sum, which is repeated until there is no carry left. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
