How can I create a to-do list in a C++ program with checkboxes for completion status? Can you provide an example of how to implement this feature?
Here is an example code snippet:
#include 
#include 
#include 
using namespace std;
struct Task {
 string name;
 bool completed;
};
int main() {
 vector tasks;
 // Add tasks to the list
 tasks.push_back({"Clean the house", false});
 tasks.push_back({"Buy groceries", false});
 tasks.push_back({"Do laundry", false});
 tasks.push_back({"Exercise", false});
 tasks.push_back({"Read a book", false});
 // Print the to-do list
 for (int i = 0; i < tasks.size(); i++) {
 cout << "- [" << (tasks[i].completed ? "x" : " ") << "] " << tasks[i].name << endl;
 }
 return 0;
}
In this example, we use a struct called "Task" to represent each item on the to-do list. The "completed" field is a boolean value that indicates whether the task has been completed or not.
We use a vector to store the list of tasks. To add tasks to the list, we use the push_back() function. To print the list, we loop through the vector and use cout to display each task, along with a checkbox that indicates its completion status.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To add checkboxes for completion status, you can modify the code as follows:
#include 
#include 
#include 
using namespace std;
struct Task {
 string name;
 bool completed;
};
int main() {
 vector tasks;
 // Add tasks to the list
 tasks.push_back({"Clean the house", false});
 tasks.push_back({"Buy groceries", false});
 tasks.push_back({"Do laundry", false});
 tasks.push_back({"Exercise", false});
 tasks.push_back({"Read a book", false});
 // Print the to-do list
 for (int i = 0; i < tasks.size(); i++) {
 cout << "- [" << (tasks[i].completed ? "x" : " ") << "] " << tasks[i].name << endl;
 }
 // Ask the user to mark tasks as completed
 int taskIndex;
 char choice;
 do {
