 for (int i = 0; i < tasks.size(); i++) {
 cout << "- [" << (tasks[i].completed ? "x" : " ") << "] " << tasks[i].name << endl;
 }
 return 0;
}
In this modified code, we first print the to-do list with checkboxes for completion status as before. Then we ask the user to enter the index of the task they completed, and mark that task as completed by setting its "completed" field to true. We then ask the user if they want to mark another task as completed, and repeat the process if they choose to do so.
Finally, we print the updated to-do list with checkboxes for completion status again. <|endoftext|>
