<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between pass by value and pass by reference in C++?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! In C++, when passing a parameter to a function, you can either pass it by value or pass it by reference. 

Passing a parameter by value means that a copy of the parameter is made and passed to the function. Any changes made to this copy inside the function will not affect the original variable outside of the function.

Passing a parameter by reference, on the other hand, means that the memory address of the original variable is passed to the function. This allows the function to directly access and modify the original variable, rather than working with a copy.

To pass a parameter by reference in C++, you use an ampersand (&) symbol before the parameter name in the function definition. For example:

void setValue(int& x) {
 x = 10;
}

In this function, we pass an integer variable "x" by reference. When the function is called and passed an integer variable as its argument, any changes made to "x" inside the function will affect the original variable outside of the function.

On the other hand, if we were to pass "x" by value instead, any changes made to "x" inside the function would only affect the copy of "x" that was created and passed to the function. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


