What is the difference between deductive and inductive reasoning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Deductive reasoning is a type of logical reasoning in which a conclusion is derived from a set of premises or assumptions, using established rules of inference. Inductive reasoning, on the other hand, involves drawing a general conclusion based on observations or specific examples. Deductive reasoning typically starts with a broad principle and then applies it to specific cases, while inductive reasoning starts with specific observations and then draws a more general conclusion based on those observations. <|endoftext|>
