<|user|>
What is the difference between inheritance and composition in Java? Can you give an example of each?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In Java, inheritance and composition are two mechanisms for achieving code reuse. Inheritance allows a subclass to inherit attributes and methods from its superclass, while composition involves creating objects of other classes within a class to reuse their functionality. 

An example of inheritance in Java would be creating a subclass "Dog" that inherits from a superclass "Animal". The "Dog" class would inherit attributes and methods such as "weight" and "eat()" from the "Animal" class.

An example of composition in Java would involve creating a class "Car" that has an instance variable of type "Engine". The "Car" class could then call methods on the "Engine" object to control the car's behavior.

In general, inheritance is used when there is an "is-a" relationship between two classes (e.g., a "Dog" is an "Animal"), while composition is used when there is a "has-a" relationship between two classes (e.g., a "Car" has an "Engine"). <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


