<|end|>
<|asst|>
In information theory, entropy is a measure of the amount of uncertainty or randomness in a system. It is often used as a measure of the average amount of information contained in each message conveyed by a communication system. The entropy of a system can be calculated based on the probabilities of the different outcomes that are possible for that system. Specifically, the entropy of a system is defined as the negative sum of the probabilities of each outcome multiplied by the logarithm of the probability of that outcome. This means that systems with more probable outcomes will have lower entropy than systems with less predictable outcomes. Thus, entropy is related to probability and uncertainty in that it provides a quantitative measure of the degree of uncertainty or unpredictability in a given system. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


