Thank you for the HTML page! Can you tell me more about the Ancient Egyptian system of fractions and how it differs from modern fractional notation?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Ancient Egyptian system of fractions was based on the unit fraction 1/n, where n is an integer. Any fraction could be expressed as a sum of these unit fractions, which allowed the Egyptians to perform complex calculations. For example, they would represent the fraction 2/5 as 1/3 + 1/15. 

This system differs from modern fractional notation, where a fraction is represented as a ratio of two integers, such as 2/5. In the Ancient Egyptian system, each fraction was written as a sum of unit fractions, with no other symbols or numbers used. Additionally, the Ancient Egyptians only used unit fractions, whereas modern fractional notation allows for any fraction to be represented. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


