What is the difference between differentiation and integration in calculus?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Differentiation and integration are two fundamental concepts in calculus. 
Differentiation involves finding the rate of change of a function with respect to its input variable, while integration involves finding the area under a curve. 
In other words, differentiation tells us how fast a function is changing at a given point, while integration tells us how much of the curve is contained within a certain region. 
They are inversely related to each other, meaning that integration "undoes" differentiation and vice versa. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
