<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! In supervised machine learning, the algorithm is trained on a labeled dataset where the desired output is known. The goal is to learn a mapping from inputs to outputs so that the algorithm can accurately predict the output for new inputs. Common algorithms include linear regression, logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, support vector machines (SVM), and neural networks.

On the other hand, unsupervised machine learning is used when there is no labeled data available. The goal is to find patterns in the data without knowing the desired output. Common algorithms include k-means clustering, hierarchical clustering, principal component analysis (PCA), and association rule mining.

There are also semi-supervised algorithms that combine elements of both supervised and unsupervised learning. These algorithms are used when only a small amount of labeled data is available but a larger amount of unlabeled data is available. The goal is to use the labeled data to improve the accuracy of predictions on the unlabeled data. One common algorithm for semi-supervised learning is self-training. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


