<|user|>
Can you explain the concept of quantum entanglement?
Noinput
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Quantum entanglement is a phenomenon in which two particles become linked in such a way that the state of one particle determines the state of the other particle, regardless of the distance between them. This is possible due to the principles of quantum mechanics, which dictate that particles can be in multiple states at once until they are observed.

For example, imagine two particles that have become entangled. If one of these particles is observed and its state is determined to be "up," then the other particle will also be "up," even if it is located on the other side of the universe. This happens instantaneously, which means that information is being transmitted faster than the speed of light, something that was previously thought to be impossible.

Scientists are still trying to fully understand the implications of quantum entanglement, but it has already been shown to have a number of practical applications, such as in quantum computing and cryptography. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


