How can I calculate the floor value of 2.2 using only bitwise operators in my C++ code? Additionally, I need the output to be in binary format and display the corresponding ASCII code for each digit in the binary value. 
Here is the C++ code:
float num = 2.2;
int floor = (int)num & ~(1 << 31);
string binary = bitset<32>(floor).to_string();
vector ascii;
for(int i=0; i<binary.length(); i++){
 ascii.push_back((int)binary[i]);
}
Can someone please explain how this code calculates the floor value using only bitwise operators? And how does it generate the binary and ASCII representations of the value? Thank you.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The code you provided uses the bitwise AND operator (&) and the bitwise NOT operator (~) to calculate the floor value of 2.2. 
In the first line, the float value 2.2 is cast to an integer using (int)num. This truncates the decimal portion of the number, effectively rounding down to the nearest integer. 
The bitwise NOT operator (~) is then applied to (1 << 31), which shifts the binary value 1 by 31 bits to the left, resulting in the binary value 10000000000000000000000000000000. The bitwise NOT operator inverts all the bits in this value, resulting in the binary value 01111111111111111111111111111111. 
Finally, the bitwise AND operator (&) is used to apply this mask to the integer value of 2.2, resulting in the floor value of 2 in binary format, which is 00000000000000000000000000000010.
The code then uses the bitset<32> function to convert the floor value to a binary string with 32 digits. The vector ascii is used to store the ASCII codes for each digit in the binary string. The for loop iterates over each digit in the binary string and converts it to its corresponding ASCII code using (int)binary[i], then adds it to the ascii vector.
So, for the floor value of 2, the binary representation is 00000000000000000000000000000010, and the corresponding ASCII codes for each digit in the binary string are 48 (for '0') and 50 (for '2'). <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
