How do hash tables work in computer science?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A hash table is a data structure that maps keys to values using a hash function. The hash function takes an input key and returns an index into an array, where the corresponding value is stored. The advantage of using a hash table is that lookups and insertions can be done in constant time on average, regardless of the size of the data set. However, collisions can occur when multiple keys map to the same index, which can lead to decreased performance if not handled properly. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


