How can you modify the given Python function to include a custom exception handling mechanism that checks if a number is even and logs an error message if it is odd? Additionally, how can you include a try-except block to handle any other exceptions that may arise in the function?
Here is the Python code:
def check_even(num):
 if num % 2 != 0:
 raise ValueError("Number is odd!")
 else:
 return True
To include custom exception handling, you can modify the function to log an error message when the number is odd. Here is the updated code:
import logging
logging.basicConfig(filename='example.log', level=logging.ERROR)
def check_even(num):
 if num % 2 != 0:
 logging.error(f"{num} is odd!")
 raise ValueError("Number is odd!")
 else:
 return True
To handle other exceptions, you can include a try-except block within the function. Here is the updated code:
import logging
logging.basicConfig(filename='example.log', level=logging.ERROR)
def check_even(num):
 try:
 if num % 2 != 0:
 logging.error(f"{num} is odd!")
 raise ValueError("Number is odd!")
 else:
 return True
 except Exception as e:
 logging.error(f"Error occurred: {e}")
 raise e
This updated function will log an error message if the number is odd, and will also handle any other exceptions that may arise.
<|end|>
