 cout << "Here are " << num_words << " descriptive words for loneliness:" << endl;
 for (int i = 0; i < num_words; i++) {
 cout << words[i] << endl;
 }
 
 return 0;
}
```
This code prompts the user to enter the number of descriptive words they want to generate for loneliness. It then checks if the number entered by the user is greater than the total number of possible descriptive words. If it is, it displays an error message. Otherwise, it sorts the vector of words in alphabetical order and removes duplicates before generating the requested number of descriptive words for loneliness. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain how the unique() function works in the modified code?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! The unique() function is used to remove consecutive duplicates from a sorted range of elements in a container. In this case, we pass it the beginning and end iterators of the "words" vector, along with the end iterator again as the third argument. This tells the function to remove all consecutive duplicates from the vector. After that, we use the erase() function to actually remove the duplicate elements from the vector. The resulting vector will only contain unique elements, which we can then sort in alphabetical order. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


