<|user|>
Is there a way to optimize the code to avoid repetition in the generated activities list?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, we can modify the implementation to ensure that there are no repeated activities in the generated list. One way to do this is to create a new list that contains all the activities from each category, shuffle that list, and then select 10 distinct activities from the shuffled list. Here's the modified implementation:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Random;

public class SummerActivities {
 private static final List OUTDOOR_ACTIVITIES = Arrays.asList(
 "Go for a hike",
 "Play frisbee in the park",
 "Have a picnic",
 "Take a bike ride",
 "Go swimming",
 "Have a BBQ",
 "Go camping",
 "Play beach volleyball",
 "Go kayaking",
 "Play soccer"
 );
 private static final List INDOOR_ACTIVITIES = Arrays.asList(
 "Watch a movie",
 "Play board games",
 "Have a video game tournament",
 "Do a puzzle",
 "Bake cookies",
 "Have a craft day",
 "Read a book",
 "Do a home workout",
 "Have a karaoke night",
 "Play hide and seek"
 );
 private static final List GROUP_ACTIVITIES = Arrays.asList(
 "Go on a group bike ride",
 "Have a game night with friends",
 "Go to an amusement park",
 "Have a potluck dinner",
 "Go on a road trip",
 "Have a bonfire",
 "Go to a concert",
 "Have a water balloon fight",
 "Go to an escape room",
 "Have a talent show"
 );
 private static final List INDIVIDUAL_ACTIVITIES = Arrays.asList(
 "Start a journal",
 "Learn a new language",
 "Take an online course",
 "Practice meditation",
 "Do a solo hike",
 "Start a garden",
 "Take up photography",
 "Write a short story",
 "Learn to play an instrument",
 "Do a DIY project"
 );
 private static final List ALL_AGES_ACTIVITIES = Arrays.asList(
 "Go to a museum",
 "Visit a zoo or aquarium",
 "Have a family game night",
 "Go to a farmers market",
 "Have a movie marathon",
 "Go to a trampoline park",
 "Have a family bike ride",
 "Go on a nature walk",
 "Visit a local attraction",
 "Have a family picnic"
 );
 
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 List allActivities = new ArrayList<>();
 allActivities.addAll(OUTDOOR_ACTIVITIES);
 allActivities.addAll(INDOOR_ACTIVITIES);
 allActivities.addAll(GROUP_ACTIVITIES);
 allActivities.addAll(INDIVIDUAL_ACTIVITIES);
 allActivities.addAll(ALL_AGES_ACTIVITIES);
 Collections.shuffle(allActivities);

 List activities = new ArrayList<>();
 for (String activity : allActivities) {
 if (activities.size() < 10 && !activities.contains(activity)) {
 activities.add(activity);
 }
 }

 System.out.println("Here are 10 summer activities to try:");
 for (String activity : activities) {
 System.out.println("- " + activity);
 }
 }
}
```

In this modified implementation, we create a new list called `allActivities` which contains all the activities from each category. We then shuffle this list using `Collections.shuffle`. Next, we create a new empty list called `activities`, and we iterate through the shuffled list. For each activity in the shuffled list, we check if the `activities` list already contains 10 activities or if the current activity is already in the `activities` list. If neither of these conditions is true, then we add the current activity to the `activities` list. Finally, we print the 10 selected activities to the console. This ensures that there are no repeated activities in the generated list. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


