<|asst|>
Ottawa was originally a small settlement called Bytown, established in 1826 to facilitate the construction of the Rideau Canal. In 1857, Queen Victoria chose Ottawa as the capital of the Province of Canada, and it became the capital of the newly-formed Dominion of Canada in 1867. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What are some popular tourist attractions in Ottawa?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Ottawa has many popular tourist attractions, including the Parliament Buildings, the Canadian Museum of History, the National Gallery of Canada, the Rideau Canal, and the Canadian War Museum. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What kind of climate does Ottawa have?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Ottawa has a humid continental climate, with warm summers and cold winters. The average temperature in July, the warmest month, is around 20°C (68°F), while the average temperature in January, the coldest month, is around -10°C (14°F). <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What are some famous festivals held in Ottawa throughout the year?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Ottawa hosts several annual festivals throughout the year, including Winterlude, a celebration of winter featuring ice sculptures and outdoor activities; the Canadian Tulip Festival, which celebrates the city's Dutch heritage with over a million tulips in bloom; and Bluesfest, a music festival featuring international and local artists. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


